## Course overview

This Language and Literature course (Group 1) develops sophisticated reading and writing in the student's first language. Students study literary and non-literary texts together, examining how **language shapes meaning** across genres, periods, and media. The course is structured around three areas of exploration: *readers, writers and texts*; *time and space*; and *intertextuality*.

## What you will study

- A range of literary works across poetry, prose, and drama.
- Non-literary text types: journalism, speeches, advertising, and digital media.
- Comparative and contextual analysis, and the global issues texts raise.

## Assessment

| Component | Weighting | Description |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Paper 1 | 35% | Guided textual analysis |
| Paper 2 | 25% | Comparative essay |
| HL Essay | 20% | 1,200–1,500 word essay (HL) |
| Individual Oral | 20% | Analysis of a global issue across two texts |

## The Individual Oral

Students analyse how a chosen global issue is presented in one literary and one non-literary text. The 10-minute oral and subsequent discussion demand independent, evidence-based argument.
